# b18 — grouping on a RANGE aggregation (issue #344).
#
# Reference of record: grafana/loki:3.7.4. The grammar half was captured
# 2026-08-02; the EXECUTION values below were captured 2026-08-04 against a
# container whose identity was READ FROM THE RUNNING PROCESS, not from any
# committed header: `/loki/api/v1/status/buildinfo` answered
# {"version":"3.7.4","revision":"b318f282","branch":"release-3.7.x"} — the
# same `b318f282` the pinned digest resolves to.
#
# WHICH OPS TAKE IT — probed, not reasoned about. All fifteen range
# aggregations were sent in both directions (`by (fp)` and `without (fp)`,
# over a log range and over an unwrapped range). Eight accept:
#
#   avg_over_time  min_over_time  max_over_time  stddev_over_time
#   stdvar_over_time  quantile_over_time  first_over_time  last_over_time
#
# Seven reject, each with `grouping not allowed for <op> aggregation`:
#
#   rate  rate_counter  count_over_time  bytes_rate  bytes_over_time
#   sum_over_time  absent_over_time
#
# `sum_over_time` and an unwrapped `rate` are the trap: both consume the
# same numeric sample stream the eight do, and both are rejected. A list
# derived from "which ones look like they should" gets those two wrong.
#
# PLACEMENT: postfix ONLY. `max_over_time by (fp) ({app="x"} | unwrap v
# [5m])` is `syntax error: unexpected by, expecting (` — the reference has
# no prefix production here, unlike its vector aggregations, which take
# both. `BY`/`WITHOUT` fold case-insensitively, as everywhere else.
#
# ORDER OF CHECKS: the grouping rejection fires BEFORE the unwrap-arity
# check. `sum_over_time({app="x"} [5m]) by (fp)` answers the grouping
# error, not `invalid aggregation sum_over_time without unwrap`; and
# `bytes_over_time({app="x"} | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)` answers the grouping
# error, not `... with unwrap`. (For an op that DOES allow grouping the
# unwrap check is reached normally: `max_over_time({app="x"} [5m]) by (fp)`
# answers `invalid aggregation max_over_time without unwrap`.)
#
# WHERE THE GROUPING IS APPLIED, and why that is the whole semantics. In
# the reference it is applied inside the SAMPLE EXTRACTOR, before a sample
# reaches any window: `streamLabelSampleExtractor.Process` ends in
# `l.builder.GroupedLabels()` (`pkg/logql/log/metrics_extraction.go:229 @
# v3.7.4`), and `batchRangeVectorIterator.load` then appends the sample
# into `window[lbs]` keyed by that already-projected label string
# (`pkg/logql/range_vector.go:149-188 @ v3.7.4`). So samples from
# DIFFERENT streams that project to the same group land in ONE sample
# slice, and the reducer sees the merged raw samples — not a per-series
# result. That is what makes this construct more than sugar for an outer
# vector aggregation, and every value below is a direct consequence.
#
# THE UNWRAPPED LABEL IS PART OF THE GROUPING, not deleted beside it.
# `LabelExtractorWithStages` (`metrics_extraction.go:154-158 @ v3.7.4`)
# folds it into the group list —
#     if len(groups) == 0 || without { without = true;
#                                      groups = append(groups, labelName) }
# — so "no grouping" and `without (…)` are one code path (both delete it)
# while `by (L)` is another (which does not). Nothing else in the
# reference ever deletes it. Hence the `by (v)` row below, where `v` IS
# the unwrapped label and survives into the output series.
#
# SEMANTICS, captured over the three streams loaded below:
#
#   * `by (L)` reduces the output labels to L alone and merges every
#     series sharing L.
#   * The merge is over the group's RAW SAMPLES. `stddev_over_time(...)
#     by (fp)` for fp=1 is the population stddev of the MERGED {1,5,7};
#     a stddev of the per-series stddevs would be 1. The second section
#     below is the sharper form of the same test.
#   * `without (L)` keeps every label except L, including parser-derived
#     ones.
#   * `by ()` collapses everything into one series with an EMPTY label
#     set; `without ()` is the identity.
#   * `by (absent_label)` collapses into `{}` — the absent name is not
#     materialised as an empty-string label.
#   * Duplicates dedupe, the same as issue #288 established for vector
#     aggregations — CONFIRMED here rather than carried over.
#
# `detected_level` is stripped from every expected label set below, per
# PROVENANCE.md: the reference injects it and PulsusDB's label model has
# no analogue. Values are unaffected.
#
# THE SAME-TIMESTAMP CROSS-STREAM TIE, on both paths. `by (fp)` on the
# first fixture creates a genuine tie: two DIFFERENT streams both hold a
# sample at 30s inside the fp="1" group ({region="eu"} v=5 and
# {region="us"} v=7). The reference resolves it by DELIVERY ORDER, and
# delivery order is `(timestamp, StableHash(stream labels))`
# (`SampleIteratorHeap.Less`, `pkg/iter/sample_iterator.go:139-148 @
# v3.7.4`; `StreamHash()` is the base-labels hash,
# `pkg/chunkenc/memchunk.go:1922 @ v3.7.4`), with `first`/`last` the ends
# of that order — `samples[0]`/`samples[len-1]` batched
# (`range_vector.go:489-501`) and `FirstOverTime.agg`/`LastOverTime.agg`
# streaming (`:818-844`). On THIS label set the `us` stream sorts first,
# so the reference answers `first` = 7 and `last` = 5.
#
# Both are asserted below, on BOTH paths. That is new with the execution
# half: the instant reducer previously used a PulsusDB-specific value
# tiebreak (smallest at the first timestamp, largest at the last), adopted
# on the premise that the reference's instant tie order was unspecified.
# The source above refutes that premise — the streaming evaluator an
# instant query uses reads the same delivery order — so the value rule was
# simply a wrong answer (7 where the reference says 5), not a ratified
# divergence, and it is gone. The one residual is unchanged and unrelated:
# same-nanosecond SAME-STREAM samples are ordered by full body bytes,
# because the reference orders them by an ingestion ordinal it does not
# store.
#
# included.
#
# provenance: captured
# (file default; see logqltest/PROVENANCE.md §provenance markers.
#  A row may override it with its own `# provenance:` line.)

load
  {env="prod", service_name="rg", fp="1", region="eu"}
  0s  v=1
  30s v=5
  {env="prod", service_name="rg", fp="2", region="eu"}
  0s  v=10
  {env="prod", service_name="rg", fp="1", region="us"}
  30s v=7

# --- the seven that reject, with the reference's wording ---

eval_fail instant at 60s rate({service_name="rg"}[1m]) by (fp)
  msg: grouping not allowed for rate aggregation

eval_fail instant at 60s rate_counter({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [1m]) by (fp)
  msg: grouping not allowed for rate_counter aggregation

eval_fail instant at 60s count_over_time({service_name="rg"}[1m]) by (fp)
  msg: grouping not allowed for count_over_time aggregation

eval_fail instant at 60s bytes_rate({service_name="rg"}[1m]) by (fp)
  msg: grouping not allowed for bytes_rate aggregation

eval_fail instant at 60s bytes_over_time({service_name="rg"}[1m]) without (fp)
  msg: grouping not allowed for bytes_over_time aggregation

# The trap case: it consumes the same sample stream the accepted eight do.
eval_fail instant at 60s sum_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [1m]) by (fp)
  msg: grouping not allowed for sum_over_time aggregation

eval_fail instant at 60s absent_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [1m]) by (fp)
  msg: grouping not allowed for absent_over_time aggregation

# The grouping rejection wins over the unwrap-arity check, both directions.
eval_fail instant at 60s sum_over_time({service_name="rg"}[1m]) by (fp)
  msg: grouping not allowed for sum_over_time aggregation

eval_fail instant at 60s bytes_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [1m]) by (fp)
  msg: grouping not allowed for bytes_over_time aggregation

# …and for an op that DOES allow grouping the unwrap check is still reached.
eval_fail instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"}[1m]) by (fp)
  msg: invalid aggregation max_over_time without unwrap

# --- placement: postfix only, so the prefix form is a plain syntax error ---

eval_fail instant at 60s max_over_time by (fp) ({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [1m])
  msg: unexpected

# --- the eight that accept it, executed ---
#
# `by (fp)` merges {region="eu"} and {region="us"} for fp="1", so its
# group holds the merged samples {1,5,7} and fp="2" holds {10}.

eval instant at 60s avg_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 4.333333333333333
  {fp="2"} 10

eval instant at 60s min_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 1
  {fp="2"} 10

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 7
  {fp="2"} 10

# THE DISCRIMINATING ROW on this fixture: 2.494438257849294 is the
# population stddev of the MERGED {1,5,7}. A per-series implementation
# (stddev of {2, 0}) gives 1.
eval instant at 60s stddev_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 2.494438257849294
  {fp="2"} 0

eval instant at 60s stdvar_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 6.222222222222222
  {fp="2"} 0

eval instant at 60s quantile_over_time(0.99, {service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 6.959999999999999
  {fp="2"} 10

eval instant at 60s quantile_over_time(0.5, {service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 5
  {fp="2"} 10

# The fp="1" group's earliest sample is the unique one at 0s, so `first`
# carries no tie here…
eval instant at 60s first_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 1
  {fp="2"} 10

# …but `last` lands squarely on the cross-stream tie at 30s (see the
# header): 5, the `eu` stream, because `us` sorts FIRST under StableHash
# and `last` takes the final delivered sample. Neither value is the
# larger of the two, which is what the deleted value tiebreak returned.
eval instant at 60s last_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 5
  {fp="2"} 10

# The same tie under `without`, so the rule is pinned for both grouping
# directions rather than only the one that reduces to a single label.
eval instant at 60s last_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) without (region)
  {env="prod", fp="1", service_name="rg"} 5
  {env="prod", fp="2", service_name="rg"} 10

# --- `without`: keeps every other label, drops the unwrapped one ---

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) without (region)
  {env="prod", fp="1", service_name="rg"} 7
  {env="prod", fp="2", service_name="rg"} 10

eval instant at 60s first_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) without (region)
  {env="prod", fp="1", service_name="rg"} 1
  {env="prod", fp="2", service_name="rg"} 10

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) without (fp, region, env)
  {service_name="rg"} 10

# --- the empty-list forms, and the ungrouped control they bracket ---

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by ()
  {} 10

# `without ()` is the identity — byte-identical to the ungrouped row below.
eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) without ()
  {env="prod", fp="1", region="eu", service_name="rg"} 5
  {env="prod", fp="1", region="us", service_name="rg"} 7
  {env="prod", fp="2", region="eu", service_name="rg"} 10

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m])
  {env="prod", fp="1", region="eu", service_name="rg"} 5
  {env="prod", fp="1", region="us", service_name="rg"} 7
  {env="prod", fp="2", region="eu", service_name="rg"} 10

# An absent name collapses to `{}` WITHOUT being materialised as `nosuch=""`.
eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (nosuch)
  {} 10

# --- multi-name and duplicate grouping lists ---

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp, region)
  {fp="1", region="eu"} 5
  {fp="1", region="us"} 7
  {fp="2", region="eu"} 10

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp, fp)
  {fp="1"} 7
  {fp="2"} 10

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) without (region, region)
  {env="prod", fp="1", service_name="rg"} 7
  {env="prod", fp="2", service_name="rg"} 10

# --- `by` on the UNWRAPPED label keeps it (see the header note) ---

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (v)
  {v="1"} 1
  {v="5"} 5
  {v="7"} 7
  {v="10"} 10

# --- case folding, and composition with an outer aggregation ---

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) BY (fp)
  {fp="1"} 7
  {fp="2"} 10

eval instant at 60s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) WITHOUT (region)
  {env="prod", fp="1", service_name="rg"} 7
  {env="prod", fp="2", service_name="rg"} 10

eval instant at 60s sum by (fp) (max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp))
  {fp="1"} 7
  {fp="2"} 10

# --- the RANGE (sliding) path takes the grouping too ------------------
#
# Grid {0,30s,60s}, window `(t-range, t]`. The fp="1" group merges the
# {region="eu"} and {region="us"} streams, so each window holds the union
# of their samples and the reducer runs over that union.
#
# `[30s]`: t=0 → fp1 {1}, fp2 {10}; t=30s → fp1 {5,7} (both streams),
# fp2 empty (a gap, the 0s sample is `≤ t-range`); t=60s → both empty.

eval range from 0s to 60s step 30s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [30s]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 0s 1 30s 7
  {fp="2"} 0s 10

eval range from 0s to 60s step 30s min_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [30s]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 0s 1 30s 5
  {fp="2"} 0s 10

# `[1m]`: t=30s → fp1 {1,5,7}; t=60s → fp1 {5,7} (0s excluded).
eval range from 0s to 60s step 30s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [1m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 0s 1 30s 7 60s 7
  {fp="2"} 0s 10 30s 10

eval range from 0s to 60s step 30s max_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [1m]) by ()
  {} 0s 10 30s 10 60s 7

# THE CROSS-STREAM TIE on the sliding path — the same rule the instant
# rows above pin, reached through a different evaluator. At t=30s the
# fp="1" window holds two samples at the SAME nanosecond from two
# DIFFERENT streams ({region="eu"} v=5 and {region="us"} v=7); the `us`
# stream sorts first under StableHash, so `first` is 7 and `last` is 5 —
# the OPPOSITE of what a value-ordered tiebreak gives for either. These
# rows are simultaneously the grouping test and the evidence that our
# `stream_hash` IS Loki's `StableHash`: nothing else would land on 7/5
# rather than 5/7.
eval range from 0s to 60s step 30s first_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [30s]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 0s 1 30s 7
  {fp="2"} 0s 10

eval range from 0s to 60s step 30s last_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [30s]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 0s 1 30s 5
  {fp="2"} 0s 10

eval range from 0s to 60s step 30s first_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [1m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 0s 1 30s 1 60s 7
  {fp="2"} 0s 10 30s 10

eval range from 0s to 60s step 30s last_over_time({service_name="rg"} | logfmt | unwrap v [1m]) by (fp)
  {fp="1"} 0s 1 30s 5 60s 5
  {fp="2"} 0s 10 30s 10

clear

# =====================================================================
# The sharper raw-sample discriminator: TWO streams sharing fp="g", with
# well-separated per-stream value ranges, so a per-series implementation
# and a merged-samples one cannot be confused for one another.
#
#   merged {1,2,10,20}: population stddev 7.628073151196178
#   per-series stddevs {0.5, 5}: their stddev would be 2.25
#
# Every sample sits at its own timestamp, so `first`/`last` carry no tie.
# =====================================================================

load
  {env="prod", service_name="rg2", fp="g", s="one"}
  0s  v=1
  15s v=2
  {env="prod", service_name="rg2", fp="g", s="two"}
  30s v=10
  45s v=20

eval instant at 60s stddev_over_time({service_name="rg2"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="g"} 7.628073151196178

# The ungrouped control: the per-series stddevs the grouped row is NOT.
eval instant at 60s stddev_over_time({service_name="rg2"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m])
  {env="prod", fp="g", s="one", service_name="rg2"} 0.5
  {env="prod", fp="g", s="two", service_name="rg2"} 5

eval instant at 60s stdvar_over_time({service_name="rg2"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="g"} 58.18749999999999

eval instant at 60s avg_over_time({service_name="rg2"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="g"} 8.25

eval instant at 60s quantile_over_time(0.5, {service_name="rg2"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="g"} 6

# first/last across the merged streams, tie-free: the group's endpoints
# are the 0s sample of one stream and the 45s sample of the other.
eval instant at 60s first_over_time({service_name="rg2"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="g"} 1

eval instant at 60s last_over_time({service_name="rg2"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp)
  {fp="g"} 20

eval instant at 60s sum(stddev_over_time({service_name="rg2"} | logfmt | unwrap v [5m]) by (fp))
  {} 7.628073151196178

clear
